      "heading": "Memory: three kinds, and where each belongs",
      "narration": "Start from the fact that makes all of this tractable: the model is stateless. It remembers nothing between calls. Every appearance of memory in every system you have ever used is your code putting something back into the context window. That is not a limitation to work around — it is the design surface, and once you see it clearly, memory stops being a mysterious capability and becomes an ordinary question about what to store and what to retrieve. Three kinds, and they want different storage. Working memory is the current context window: the conversation as it stands. It is fast and exact and it ends when the request does. The design problem here is what to do when it fills up. The wrong answer, and the common one, is to truncate from the front — which silently deletes the beginning of the conversation, including whatever the user asked for and whatever the model agreed to, and produces an assistant that contradicts itself for no visible reason. The better approach is to keep the last several turns verbatim, because recency matters most, and replace older turns with a running summary. And keep the system prompt pinned regardless, because dropping your own instructions is a spectacular way to fail. Episodic memory is what happened in previous sessions: past conversations, past agent runs, past decisions. This is not something the context can hold, so it goes in storage — and the retrieval machinery is exactly RAG, pointed at your own history instead of at documents. Embed each past exchange, and when a new request arrives, retrieve the handful of past exchanges that are relevant to it and put those in the prompt. Note the useful consequence: you do not need a longer context window to have a long memory. You need good retrieval over history, which is cheaper and scales further. Semantic memory is different in kind, and this is where people make an architectural mistake worth avoiding. These are durable extracted facts: the user's name, their timezone, that they prefer terse answers, that they are allergic to something, that their account is on the enterprise plan. Do not store these as free text in a vector database. Store them as structured records in an ordinary database — fields with names and values. The reason is not performance. It is that structured records can be read by a human, corrected when wrong, deleted on request, and audited. A fact about a user living as an embedding in a vector store is a fact you cannot easily show them, cannot easily fix, and cannot reliably delete, and every one of those is a requirement in most jurisdictions and every serious product. The rule to carry: retrieve unstructured history, but write down structured facts.",
