      "heading": "Evaluate the trajectory, not just the answer",
      "narration": "Evaluating an agent is genuinely harder than evaluating a classifier, and the reason is that the same final answer can be produced by a good process or by an accident. If your agent called the wrong tool, got lucky, called another wrong tool, and then guessed something correct, you have a passing test and a system that will fail the next time in a way you have no warning about. So you evaluate the trajectory. Look at the sequence of steps: were the right tools chosen, were they called in a sensible order, were the arguments correct, did each step build on the last, and did the agent stop when it had the answer rather than continuing to poke at things. A right answer reached by a wrong route is a broken system that has not failed yet. Four metrics are worth the effort of collecting. Task success rate, defined against real tasks rather than made-up ones. Tool-choice accuracy, measured per step, because that is where most agent failures actually originate and it tells you which tool descriptions need rewriting. Steps to completion, because an agent taking twelve steps for a three-step task is telling you something is wrong with its reasoning even when it eventually succeeds. And cost per successful task — not cost per call, cost per success, because a system that succeeds half the time at two dollars a run costs four dollars per useful outcome, and that is the number that determines whether it ships. Grading trajectories by hand does not scale past a few dozen, so LLM-as-judge is the standard approach: another model reads the trace and scores it. It works, and it needs the same discipline as any other measurement instrument. Write an explicit rubric with the criteria spelled out rather than asking is this good. Pin the judge model and version, because your scores are not comparable across a judge upgrade. And periodically check the judge against human labels on a sample — if it agrees with you eighty percent of the time you can use it for relative comparisons but not for absolute claims. An uncalibrated judge produces a number, and a number is not evidence. And the practical instruction that matters most: build the regression set before you start optimizing. Fifty real tasks, with the correct answer and a known-good trajectory for each. Run it on every prompt change, every model swap, every new tool. Without it, you will improve one case, break three you were not looking at, and have no way to know — and the LLM-specific version of this is worse than usual, because prompt changes have non-local effects. Every team that ships reliable agents has this file. Every team that does not is guessing and calling it iteration.",
